    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          EGO makes the most complete battery snow lineup on the market, and that is exactly why buyers pick the wrong machine. The 21-inch single-stage is the right answer for most paved driveways. The 24-inch two-stage is worth its much higher price only if you have a plow ridge, gravel, or regular storms over eight inches. The shovel and the attachment are for steps and walks, not driveways. Get that choice right and the batteries become the only real decision left.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The EGO Snow Lineup at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            EGO sells snow clearing in four forms, all running on the same 56V ARC Lithium batteries that power its mowers, blowers and trimmers. There is a 21-inch single-stage snow blower, a 24-inch two-stage self-propelled snow blower, a dedicated cordless snow shovel, and a shovel attachment that clips onto the Power+ Multi-Head System power head. Model numbers shift from season to season as EGO bundles different batteries and chargers into kits, so the clearest way to think about the range is by class rather than by part number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two snow blowers are the ones that matter for driveways. The single-stage design uses a rubber-edged auger that scrapes the pavement and throws snow out of the chute in one motion. The two-stage design uses a steel auger to feed a separate high-speed impeller, which is what lets it chew through compacted, wet and icy snow that would stop a single-stage. That difference in mechanism, not the extra three inches of width, is what you are paying for when you step up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shovel and the attachment are a different category entirely. They clear a narrow path, they are light enough to carry up a staircase, and they suit a deck, a front walk or a short path to the mailbox. Neither is a substitute for a snow blower on a two-car driveway, and buying one in the hope that it will be is the most common EGO snow mistake we see in owner reviews.
          </p>

          <p>
            We rank EGO's two snow blowers on our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">best electric snow blowers</a> list, where the 24-inch two-stage SNT2405 takes Best Overall and the 21-inch single-stage SNT2112 takes Best Compact. This page is the companion to that list. It explains how the rest of the lineup fits around those two picks and helps you decide which one, if any, belongs in your garage.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">EGO model class</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Clearing width</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Not for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">21-inch single-stage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paved driveways, snow up to about 8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gravel, plow ridges, deep wet snow</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24-inch two-stage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plow ridges, gravel, frequent heavy storms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tight budgets, small walks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cordless snow shovel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Narrow, shovel-width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Steps, decks, short walks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Driveways</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power head shovel attachment</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Narrow, shovel-width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Owners already on the Multi-Head system</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anyone buying it as their main tool</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="single-stage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 21-Inch Single-Stage: The One Most Driveways Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a paved driveway in a region that sees regular snowfall of three to eight inches, the 21-inch single-stage is the EGO to buy. It is light enough to lift onto a porch, compact enough to store beside a car, and quick to turn around obstacles. Because the auger scrapes the surface, it leaves cleaner pavement than a two-stage machine, which rides on skid shoes and tends to leave a thin packed layer behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            The version we rank, the SNT2112, runs on two 56V batteries and earned its Best Compact spot on throwing distance and maneuverability. EGO has sold several variants of this 21-inch machine, some bundled with different battery sizes and some with a single battery port, so check the listing for how many batteries the machine takes and how many are included in the box. That detail changes both the price and the runtime more than any other spec on the page.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single-stage has firm limits, and they are the same limits every single-stage machine has, gas or battery. Its practical depth ceiling is around eight inches. It cannot be used on gravel, because the scraping auger picks up stones and throws them. And it will ride up over the compacted, partly frozen ridge that a municipal plow leaves across the end of your driveway rather than breaking through it. If that ridge is a weekly event where you live, you will be finishing it with a hand shovel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runtime is the other constraint. On our pillar list we put it at 30 to 45 minutes depending on snow conditions and temperature, which covers a typical two-car driveway with margin. Variable speed control helps here: running the auger slower on light powder stretches the battery, and saving full speed for wet, heavy snow keeps you from draining the packs on the easy part of the job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Paved, under eight inches, no plow ridge: buy the single-stage.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If all three are true for your driveway most winters, the 21-inch single-stage does the job for far less money than the two-stage, and it is easier to live with. If any one of them is false most winters, keep reading.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="two-stage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The 24-Inch Two-Stage: When It Is Worth the Money
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 24-inch two-stage self-propelled machine is the model that made battery snow blowers a serious alternative to gas. The SNT2405 tops our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">electric snow blower rankings</a> because it is a real two-stage design: a steel auger feeds a separate impeller, so the machine breaks into compacted snow rather than skating over it. Adjustable skid shoes let it run on gravel, which no single-stage electric machine can do safely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Self-propulsion matters more on a two-stage than on a single-stage, because these machines are heavy. You steer and the drive does the pushing, which is the difference between an easy morning and a sore back on a sloped driveway. EGO also fits LED headlights to its snow blowers, a small feature that earns its place when you are clearing before sunrise in December.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case against it is cost and runtime. Two-stage machines draw more power, so they run on two batteries and our pillar puts realistic runtime at 45 to 60 minutes, less in a deep freeze. Once you add enough batteries and a rapid charger to clear a long driveway after a heavy storm without stopping, the total sits at premium pricing, often well above a capable gas two-stage. You are paying for push-button starts, no fuel, no carburetor and much less noise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the two-stage if you face a plow ridge most storms, have a gravel driveway, regularly see more than eight inches at a time, or have a driveway long enough that a single-stage would mean several passes through deep snow. If none of those apply, the extra money buys capability you will rarely use. If several apply and your driveway is very long, read our <a href="/electric-vs-gas-snow-blower/">electric vs gas snow blower</a> comparison before deciding, because a gas two-stage may simply be the better tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth comparing with Toro's 60V two-stage before you commit. Toro's machine ranks as our best two-stage alternative and comes with a dealer network for service, while EGO wins on how many other tools share its batteries. Our <a href="/toro-electric-snow-blower/">Toro electric snow blower guide</a> covers that lineup in the same way this page covers EGO's.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shovels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Snow Shovel and the Power Head Attachment
          </h2>

          <p>
            EGO's cordless snow shovel is a single-battery, push-style tool with a short rotating paddle at the front. It is built for the jobs a snow blower is clumsy at: steps, decks, porches, narrow walkways and the path from the back door to the trash cans. It is light enough to carry up and down stairs, and it throws snow a modest distance to the side rather than across the yard.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shovel attachment does the same job in a different form. It clips onto the EGO Power+ Multi-Head System power head, the motor unit that also drives EGO's string trimmer, edger, pole saw and other attachments. If you already own that power head, the attachment is the cheapest way into EGO snow clearing. If you do not, buying the power head just to clear snow makes little sense, because the dedicated shovel is simpler and handles better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither tool clears a driveway well. A shovel-width path takes many passes to cover a two-car driveway, it struggles once snow gets deeper than a few inches, and wet, heavy snow slows it badly. Owners who are happy with these tools almost always use them alongside a snow blower or on properties with only a walk to clear. Owners who are unhappy almost always bought one to replace a snow blower.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a powered shovel is what you are after, our <a href="/electric-snow-shovel/">electric snow shovel guide</a> compares EGO's shovel with the corded and cordless options from Snow Joe and others, including what clearing width and battery size actually change. For EGO owners specifically, the shovel is the obvious second tool: it uses the batteries you already have and covers the steps the snow blower cannot reach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Batteries: The Part of the Price Nobody Quotes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sticker price of an EGO snow blower depends heavily on what is in the box. The same machine can be sold bare, with two smaller packs, or with two large packs and a rapid charger, and the gap between those kits can be hundreds of dollars. Before comparing EGO with anything else, price the kit you would actually need for your driveway, not the cheapest listing with the model name on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            EGO's 56V packs come in a wide range of capacities, from small packs suited to trimmers up to large packs intended for mowers and snow blowers. For snow, bigger is better: a larger pack runs longer and is less affected by cold than a small one under the same load. If you already own EGO batteries from a mower or blower, check their capacity. Two small trimmer packs will run a snow blower, but not for long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold is the hidden cost. Lithium-ion cells deliver less energy when they are cold, which is why every runtime figure on our pillar list carries the caveat of less in severe cold. Store your batteries indoors, not in an unheated garage, and put them in the machine just before you head out. Do not try to charge a pack that has just come in from below freezing; let it warm to room temperature first. EGO chargers are designed to hold off charging when a pack is too cold, so a charger that seems to do nothing on a cold pack is usually protecting it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The upside of the platform is sharing. The batteries that clear your driveway in January run your mower in June, which is the strongest argument for EGO over a smaller platform. If you already own an EGO mower with large packs, a bare-tool snow blower can be a very reasonable buy. If you own nothing EGO yet, compare the full kit price against Toro and Snow Joe; our <a href="/snow-joe-snow-blower/">Snow Joe snow blower guide</a> covers the budget end of that comparison.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Runtime From Our Pillar Rankings</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30-45 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">21-inch single-stage</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">45-60 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">24-inch two-stage</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">56V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Shared ARC Lithium platform</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Less</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">In severe cold, on any model</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="shortfalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where EGO Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            EGO's biggest weakness is the one shared by every battery snow blower: energy. A gas two-stage with a full tank runs for well over an hour and refuels in a minute. An EGO runs until the packs are flat and then needs either spare packs or a long recharge. After a big storm on a long driveway, that is a real limitation, and buying enough batteries to remove it is expensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Service is the second weakness. Battery tools have fewer parts to fail than gas engines, and owners commonly report that EGO machines need little beyond basic care, but when something does fail, support runs through EGO's warranty process and service centers rather than the kind of local small-engine dealer that Toro, Ariens and Honda owners can walk into. If having a nearby shop matters to you, factor that in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight and storage are the third. The 24-inch two-stage is a heavy machine that needs real garage floor space, and it is not something you lift onto a porch. The single-stage is far easier to handle, but that is precisely because it gives up the capability that makes the two-stage worth owning. There is no EGO that combines two-stage capability with single-stage convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            On warranty, EGO has historically offered a multi-year limited warranty on its outdoor power tools and a separate term for batteries and chargers, with shorter coverage for commercial use. Terms change and vary by product, so check the warranty for the specific model and battery you are buying, and register the product when you get it. Keep the receipt with the manual, because a battery warranty claim usually needs proof of purchase.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST TAKE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">EGO beats gas on convenience, not on stamina.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Push-button starts, no fuel and little maintenance are real advantages for a typical suburban driveway. For a long driveway in a heavy-snow region, a gas two-stage still clears more snow per hour and per dollar.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which EGO to Buy, by Driveway
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the surface. A gravel driveway rules out the single-stage, the shovel and the attachment, which leaves the 24-inch two-stage as the only EGO option. If that machine is beyond your budget, a gas two-stage from our <a href="/best-snow-blower/">best snow blowers</a> list will cost less and clear gravel just as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a paved driveway, look at your typical storm and whether the plow leaves a ridge. Up to about eight inches of ordinary snow with no regular ridge points to the 21-inch single-stage. Frequent deep storms, heavy wet snow or a weekly plow ridge point to the 24-inch two-stage. Most suburban buyers in moderate snow regions land on the single-stage and are glad they did.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add the shovel only if you have steps, a deck or a long walk that the snow blower cannot reach easily. If you already own the Multi-Head power head, the attachment is the cheaper route; otherwise the dedicated shovel is the better tool. A household with a short walk and no driveway, such as a townhouse with off-street parking, may need nothing more than the shovel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, price the batteries honestly. If you already own large EGO packs, a bare-tool machine is excellent value. If you own nothing, the full kit price is the number to compare against Toro and Snow Joe. Whatever you buy, give it the same preseason check our <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">snow blower winter prep guide</a> describes: inspect the auger and paddles, check the skid shoes, charge every pack, and run it once before the first storm.
          </p>

