        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the shovel to clear a driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The EGO snow shovel and the shovel attachment clear a narrow path and slow down badly in deep or wet snow. They are excellent on steps and walks and frustrating on a two-car driveway. If the driveway is the main job, buy a snow blower and treat the shovel as a second tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing bare-tool prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                EGO machines are sold bare and in kits with very different battery sizes. A cheap listing may include no batteries or small ones that barely finish a driveway. Price the kit you would actually need, including a charger, before comparing EGO against Toro, Snow Joe or a gas machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a single-stage on gravel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single-stage auger scrapes the surface, so on gravel it picks up stones and throws them at windows, cars and people. It also wears the paddles quickly. Gravel driveways need the 24-inch two-stage with its skid shoes set to lift the housing, or a gas two-stage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing batteries in a cold garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium-ion packs lose usable energy in the cold, and a pack left overnight in a freezing garage starts the job already handicapped. Keep packs indoors at room temperature, load them just before clearing, and let cold packs warm up before charging them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overbuying the two-stage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 24-inch two-stage is the better machine, but on a paved driveway with ordinary storms it is heavier, slower to maneuver and much more expensive than the job requires. Unless you face plow ridges, gravel or frequent deep snow, the single-stage is the smarter buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which EGO snow blower is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most paved driveways with storms under about eight inches, the 21-inch single-stage is the best EGO, and it is our Best Compact pick. If you have a plow ridge, gravel or frequent heavy snow, the 24-inch two-stage self-propelled SNT2405 is better, and it is our Best Overall electric snow blower. Price the full battery kit before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an EGO snow blower run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On our pillar rankings we put the 21-inch single-stage at roughly 30 to 45 minutes and the 24-inch two-stage at roughly 45 to 60 minutes, depending on snow and battery size. Severe cold cuts both. Larger packs and storing them indoors stretch runtime more than anything else you can do.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an EGO snow blower handle a plow ridge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The 24-inch two-stage can, because its steel auger and impeller break into compacted snow. The 21-inch single-stage generally cannot; it tends to ride up over the hard ridge left by a municipal plow. If that ridge appears after most storms where you live, the two-stage is the EGO to buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an EGO snow blower on gravel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only the 24-inch two-stage, with its skid shoes adjusted to lift the auger housing above the stones. The single-stage, the snow shovel and the shovel attachment all scrape the surface and will throw gravel. Never use a single-stage machine on a gravel driveway, from EGO or anyone else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do EGO mower batteries work in the snow blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. EGO's snow blowers, shovel and attachment all run on the same 56V ARC Lithium batteries as its mowers, blowers and trimmers. Larger packs are the better match for snow clearing. Small trimmer packs will run a snow blower, but runtime will be short, especially in cold weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the EGO snow shovel worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it for steps, decks, porches and short walks, especially if you already own EGO batteries. It is not worth it as a driveway tool. The narrow path and limited power in deep or wet snow make it slow on anything larger than a walkway, so treat it as a companion to a snow blower.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is EGO better than gas for snow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical suburban paved driveway, EGO is easier to live with: push-button starts, no fuel, less noise and little maintenance. For a very long driveway in a heavy-snow region, gas still wins on runtime and on cost per hour of clearing. Our electric vs gas comparison walks through that decision in detail.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

